course: dictionary
lesson: 7574
title: Words about leaders
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Presidential» is a long word, but its clues are close by. Look at the word next to it. In «a presidential election», the word election tells you people are voting, so presidential must be about a president.
explain: The word can also describe how someone looks or acts. If something looks serious and important, the way a president should, it can be called presidential. The clues come from the way the word is used, not from a list of rules.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. In «the US presidential election», put "to do with a president" in place of presidential. «The US election to do with a president» still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Presidential means to do with a president, the elected leader of a country.
fact: «A presidential election» is a vote to choose a president.
fact: In the USA, presidential elections happen every four years, and the winner lives in the White House.
fact: In Ireland, the President is head of state, but the Taoiseach leads the government.
fact: Presidential can also mean serious and important, the way a president should look or act.
exercise: «In the US presidential election, each state gives its votes to the winner in that state.» What does presidential mean here? | Very hot and sunny | Happening in a school playground | Only about money | *To do with choosing a president
exercise: In Ireland, the President is head of state, but the Taoiseach leads the government. Which job leads the government? | President | *Taoiseach | Head of the school | Mayor of the town
exercise: Ana says that if someone looks presidential, they look serious and important, as a president should. Is she right? | No, it means someone is asleep | *Yes, presidential can mean serious and important | No, it means someone is hungry | No, it only means very tiny
test: «After a falsified presidential election (one whose votes were counted dishonestly) in November 2004, hundreds of thousands protested peacefully.» What does falsified mean here? | A fair vote with a clear winner | *Votes were counted dishonestly | Votes were counted very quickly | Nobody came to vote
test: «When votes are won group by group, as in the US Electoral College, a candidate can win the most groups with fewer votes in total.» Why can this happen? | Because the groups are all the same size | *Because votes are won group by group, not by adding up every vote | Because nobody voted at all | Because the candidate has the most votes in total
exam: In the line «presidential candidates debate on TV», who are the candidates? | Cameramen filming the debate | Newsreaders reading the news | Students voting for class captain | *People wanting to become president
